WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.320
What's up? Welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners, Episode 52 from May 27th, 2026.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:11.000
We've got some really cool AI stuff to talk about today. Let's dive in.

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:16.760
So imagine you are working on a big school research project and you ask two different friends

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:22.100
for help. One friend always checks their facts twice and shows you exactly where they found

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:27.040
the information. The other friend sounds super confident, but sometimes just makes things

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:33.340
up on the spot. A new study just ranked AI chatbots the same way, and perplexity came out

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:39.080
on top while chat GPT landed in sixth place for trustworthy answers on real work tasks.

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:44.520
Think of perplexity like that careful friend because it pulls fresh information from the

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:51.460
web and lists its sources every single time you ask a question. Chat GPT can be great for

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:56.720
brainstorming story ideas or writing creative drafts, but the study found it sometimes

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:02.020
gives answers that sound right without being accurate. This matters because more students

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:06.920
and teens are using these tools for homework, college applications, and learning new skills

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:12.440
every day. When an AI gives you wrong facts, you can end up with bad information that affects

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:17.900
your grades or how well you understand the topic. The gap between the top and lower ranked

00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:22.880
chatbots shows that not all AI tools are equal when you need answers you can count on.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:28.700
For you personally, this means the chatbot you pick can change how much time you spend double

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:35.180
checking its work later. If you are using AI to help with essays or research, starting with a more

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:41.640
reliable one saves you from fixing mistakes down the line. You can try perplexity right now for free

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:47.440
by going to the perplexity website on your phone or laptop. Ask it a question about something you're

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:52.980
studying like what caused the fall of the Roman Empire and notice how it lists sources at the

00:01:52.980 --> 00:01:59.300
bottom so you can check them yourself. Now for my favorite part of the show, The Deep Dive,

00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:04.860
where we look under the hood. You know how when you are texting a friend, your phone sometimes guesses

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:10.460
the next word before you finish typing. That is basically what modern AI chatbots are doing,

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:15.860
except the autocomplete got millions of times smarter because it studied billions of sentences

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:22.540
instead of just your messages. Imagine the AI breaks every sentence into tiny pieces called tokens,

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:29.220
which are basically word chunks like syllables or short phrases. It looks at how those chunks usually

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:34.500
connect to each other across all the text it has seen before. Then it predicts what chunks should

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:40.240
come next based only on patterns not because it understands meaning the way a person does.

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:46.560
The system keeps doing this over and over, building full answers one token at a time while

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:52.200
checking what it has already written so the whole thing stays consistent. It never thinks or has

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:56.760
opinions. It just calculates which token is most likely to fit the pattern it learned.

00:02:57.500 --> 00:03:03.340
So next time someone says an AI is reasoning, you can tell them it is basically super powered autocomplete

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:09.480
running on patterns, not actual understanding. And that is basically how these chatbots work.

00:03:09.480 --> 00:03:15.100
Not so scary once you see the steps, right? Now let's look at some cool stuff you can try today.

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:20.780
People are leaving Google search in big numbers because they do not like AI answers mixed

00:03:20.780 --> 00:03:26.980
into the results. Google changed its search so an AI writes a big summary at the top instead of

00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:33.440
just showing normal links. A lot of users felt forced into using AI even when they just wanted regular

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:41.320
websites. DuckDuckGo downloads jumped 30% almost overnight as a result. If you want a search engine

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:48.120
that still gives you plain links without the AI summary, DuckDuckGo is a simple switch. Open the DuckDuckGo

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:53.380
website in your browser or download the app and try searching for something you care about like

00:03:53.380 --> 00:03:59.760
your favorite band or homework topic to see the difference in how results look. Next up is something fun

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:05.720
for anyone who makes music or just likes experimenting with sounds. Music labels are teaming up with

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:12.240
TikTok to stop AI from making fake songs that copy real artists without permission. This helps protect

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:18.520
creators from having their style stolen by AI tools. You can explore this yourself by trying free

00:04:18.520 --> 00:04:26.240
ethical AI music tools that do not copy specific artists. Go to the Suno website, type a simple prompt

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:32.100
like upbeat low five beat for studying and listen to what it creates without using anyone's real

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:39.980
voice or style. Time for quick bits. The RapidFire section. A tiny software bug called Badhost was

00:04:39.980 --> 00:04:47.040
found in a popular free tool called Starlet that many AI projects use. Even though it sounds technical,

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:52.480
it shows why keeping the software behind AI tools updated matters for everyone who uses them.

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:57.840
Human therapists already have a legal duty to report users who talk about hurting people.

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:04.580
Some experts think the same rules should apply to AI chatbots. It raises big questions about privacy

00:05:04.580 --> 00:05:10.580
versus safety that will affect how chatbots are designed in the future. That's it for today. Remember

00:05:10.580 --> 00:05:16.420
every AI expert started exactly where you are right now. If something we talked about today made

00:05:16.420 --> 00:05:22.900
you curious, go try it. That is literally how learning works. Stay curious, keep experimenting,

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:28.460
and we'll see you tomorrow. And if you'd rather watch than listen, find us on YouTube at

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:34.860
Nerra Network, links in the show notes. This episode used AI voice synthesis of my voice.

00:05:35.480 --> 00:05:38.020
Editorial selection and analysis are my own.
